    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d566">

                <pb facs="0623" n="519" sameAs="frus:frus1913/pg519"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 822.124/317.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Quito</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1913-11-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 17, 1913</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The representative of J. G. White &amp; Co. says that his company desires to meet
                    the views of our Government and that Engineer Williamson, formerly with the
                    Isthmian Canal Commission, will be in charge of the work; that his company
                    desires to have the cooperation of our Government and that Gorgas stands at the
                    head of the list in sanitary work. In the absence of instructions to the
                    contrary I am proceeding on the assumption that the Department desires above all
                    else the speedy and successful sanitation of Guayaquil, and, everything being
                    equal, that it prefers to have the work done by American contractors.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hartman</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
